1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,558
Add up all the money Americans have loaded into the Starbucks app.

2
00:00:03,558 --> 00:00:04,310
How much is it?

3
00:00:05,130 --> 00:00:08,890
About 1.75 billion dollars.

4
00:00:09,710 --> 00:00:11,526
After the holiday gift season,

5
00:00:11,526 --> 00:00:13,100
it tops 2 billion dollars.

6
00:00:13,920 --> 00:00:17,040
If you own Starbucks stock or are thinking about it,

7
00:00:17,260 --> 00:00:19,318
the next nine minutes will show you,

8
00:00:19,318 --> 00:00:20,404
in one big picture,

9
00:00:20,404 --> 00:00:22,290
how Starbucks really makes money.

10
00:00:23,110 --> 00:00:25,210
Here's what you'll take away.

11
00:00:25,430 --> 00:00:28,280
One: the money behind Starbucks that isn't coffee.

12
00:00:28,490 --> 00:00:31,450
Two: why that money is worth more than interest.

13
00:00:31,670 --> 00:00:35,076
Three: the three numbers to watch on October 29 earnings,

14
00:00:35,076 --> 00:00:36,570
and why only those three.

15
00:00:37,390 --> 00:00:39,132
If a bank held this money,

16
00:00:39,132 --> 00:00:42,280
it would owe you interest. Starbucks pays none.

17
00:00:42,490 --> 00:00:44,418
Zero interest, no due date.

18
00:00:44,418 --> 00:00:46,560
I'd take a loan like that too.

19
00:00:47,390 --> 00:00:49,620
First, where does this money come from?

20
00:00:50,440 --> 00:00:53,836
Starbucks has 41,304 stores worldwide,

21
00:00:53,836 --> 00:00:56,160
16,933 of them in the U.S.

22
00:00:56,370 --> 00:00:59,003
Picture a weekday morning in America.

23
00:00:59,003 --> 00:01:02,560
You order ahead in the app and just grab your cup.

24
00:01:02,780 --> 00:01:03,933
When the balance runs low,

25
00:01:03,933 --> 00:01:05,750
the app reloads 25 dollars automatically.

26
00:01:06,570 --> 00:01:09,870
35.8 million active Starbucks Rewards members in the U.S.

27
00:01:10,080 --> 00:01:12,676
They account for 59% of spending at U.S.

28
00:01:12,676 --> 00:01:14,234
company-operated stores.

29
00:01:14,234 --> 00:01:16,830
Six in ten dollars come through the app.

30
00:01:17,060 --> 00:01:20,538
Members earn Stars with every purchase and redeem them for drinks,

31
00:01:20,538 --> 00:01:21,750
so they keep reloading.

32
00:01:22,560 --> 00:01:23,764
In one quarter,

33
00:01:23,764 --> 00:01:28,180
3.1 billion dollars were loaded onto cards and the app.

34
00:01:28,400 --> 00:01:31,190
The money arrives before the coffee is even made.

35
00:01:32,010 --> 00:01:34,896
Starbucks launched its prepaid card in 2001.

36
00:01:34,896 --> 00:01:36,076
Two decades later,

37
00:01:36,076 --> 00:01:38,700
a coffee company has a bank-like wallet.

38
00:01:38,920 --> 00:01:40,407
Today one in three orders at U.S.

39
00:01:40,407 --> 00:01:42,389
company-operated stores comes in through the

40
00:01:42,389 --> 00:01:43,830
app before the customer arrives.

41
00:01:44,060 --> 00:01:45,084
By transactions,

42
00:01:45,084 --> 00:01:48,282
42% are paid with a Starbucks Card or app balance.

43
00:01:48,282 --> 00:01:49,050
Nearly half.

44
00:01:49,870 --> 00:01:52,958
Korea is no different: Siren Order balances

45
00:01:52,958 --> 00:01:56,190
reached 427.5 billion won at the end of 2025.

46
00:01:56,410 --> 00:01:58,112
But today is about the U.S. parent,

47
00:01:58,112 --> 00:02:00,300
the Starbucks you can actually buy shares in.

48
00:02:01,120 --> 00:02:03,320
So what is this money, in accounting terms?

49
00:02:04,140 --> 00:02:05,080
To Starbucks,

50
00:02:05,080 --> 00:02:07,610
your loaded balance is a liability.

51
00:02:07,830 --> 00:02:10,520
It takes the money now and hands over coffee later.

52
00:02:10,730 --> 00:02:13,379
But this debt has no interest and no due date.

53
00:02:13,379 --> 00:02:14,703
Customers are the bank,

54
00:02:14,703 --> 00:02:16,200
Starbucks is the borrower.

55
00:02:16,420 --> 00:02:17,896
It is coffee sold in advance,

56
00:02:17,896 --> 00:02:19,780
cash Starbucks can put to work first.

57
00:02:20,610 --> 00:02:21,356
Back in 2016,

58
00:02:21,356 --> 00:02:24,570
analysts noted Starbucks cards held 1.2 billion dollars,

59
00:02:24,570 --> 00:02:26,980
more than many U.S. banks had in deposits.

60
00:02:27,200 --> 00:02:29,720
Today it's over 2 billion after the holidays.

61
00:02:29,930 --> 00:02:32,984
The difference: bank deposits earn interest,

62
00:02:32,984 --> 00:02:34,720
Starbucks balances don't.

63
00:02:35,550 --> 00:02:37,996
Loading has seasons: the quarter with the

64
00:02:37,996 --> 00:02:40,620
holidays brings in over 3.5 billion dollars.

65
00:02:40,830 --> 00:02:43,834
Gift cards pile up as balances into the new year,

66
00:02:43,834 --> 00:02:46,470
so the balance peaks right after Christmas.

67
00:02:47,290 --> 00:02:51,100
Say Starbucks borrowed it from a bank at just 4%,

68
00:02:51,100 --> 00:02:52,810
just as an assumption.

69
00:02:53,030 --> 00:02:57,330
That's about 70 million dollars a year in interest it never pays.

70
00:02:57,560 --> 00:03:00,550
So is Starbucks really in the interest business?

71
00:03:01,370 --> 00:03:06,340
Promise one: is the money behind Starbucks really interest?

72
00:03:07,150 --> 00:03:10,642
How much can 1.75 billion dollars earn in a year?

73
00:03:10,642 --> 00:03:12,210
Less than you'd think.

74
00:03:13,030 --> 00:03:15,560
Let's open the books. In fiscal 2025,

75
00:03:15,560 --> 00:03:19,390
Starbucks made 2.94 billion dollars of operating income.

76
00:03:19,610 --> 00:03:22,221
All its interest and other income,

77
00:03:22,221 --> 00:03:25,600
from all its cash combined, was 113 million.

78
00:03:25,820 --> 00:03:27,583
About 4% of operating income.

79
00:03:27,583 --> 00:03:29,710
Starbucks doesn't live on interest.

80
00:03:29,930 --> 00:03:31,810
Customers can spend it any time,

81
00:03:31,810 --> 00:03:33,808
so it can't be locked up for long.

82
00:03:33,808 --> 00:03:36,980
The structure was never built for big interest income.

83
00:03:37,800 --> 00:03:39,810
But the books show another line,

84
00:03:39,810 --> 00:03:41,380
twice as big as interest.

85
00:03:42,200 --> 00:03:45,450
Got a forgotten gift card in a drawer somewhere?

86
00:03:46,270 --> 00:03:47,393
From past patterns,

87
00:03:47,393 --> 00:03:50,290
Starbucks estimates how much will never be spent.

88
00:03:50,510 --> 00:03:55,060
It books that as revenue. It's called breakage.

89
00:03:55,280 --> 00:03:56,445
It's an estimate,

90
00:03:56,445 --> 00:03:59,460
adjusted later if actual redemptions differ.

91
00:04:00,280 --> 00:04:06,160
Fiscal 2025 breakage was 222 million dollars.

92
00:04:06,380 --> 00:04:08,813
About twice the interest and other income.

93
00:04:08,813 --> 00:04:11,420
Forgotten balances earned more than interest.

94
00:04:11,640 --> 00:04:12,257
Honestly,

95
00:04:12,257 --> 00:04:15,410
I have a card with a balance in my drawer too.

96
00:04:15,410 --> 00:04:16,850
I guess I chipped in.

97
00:04:17,070 --> 00:04:19,942
Still under 8% of operating income.

98
00:04:19,942 --> 00:04:22,650
So the real power lies elsewhere.

99
00:04:23,470 --> 00:04:26,610
Promise two: why that money beats interest.

100
00:04:27,440 --> 00:04:29,139
With 12 dollars left in the app,

101
00:04:29,139 --> 00:04:31,210
it's hard to try another cafe tomorrow.

102
00:04:31,420 --> 00:04:33,996
The real power isn't interest.

103
00:04:33,996 --> 00:04:37,603
It's the string that pulls customers back.

104
00:04:37,603 --> 00:04:38,290
Lock-in.

105
00:04:39,110 --> 00:04:43,438
But the string holds only when the coffee experience does.

106
00:04:43,438 --> 00:04:46,870
In fiscal 2025, U.S. comparable sales fell 2%.

107
00:04:47,090 --> 00:04:50,225
CEO Brian Niccol, who arrived in September 2024,

108
00:04:50,225 --> 00:04:54,210
launched Back to Starbucks to fix the store experience first.

109
00:04:54,430 --> 00:04:57,888
While it trimmed the menu and reworked store service,

110
00:04:57,888 --> 00:05:00,889
the fiscal 2025 operating margin fell to 7.9%.

111
00:05:00,889 --> 00:05:02,650
The fixing cost came first.

112
00:05:02,870 --> 00:05:04,570
And last quarter, U.S.

113
00:05:04,570 --> 00:05:07,120
comparable store sales rose 7.9%.

114
00:05:07,940 --> 00:05:11,860
What matters is why: transactions rose 4.2%.

115
00:05:12,090 --> 00:05:15,436
Active Rewards members also grew from 33.8 to

116
00:05:15,436 --> 00:05:17,369
35.8 million in two years.

117
00:05:17,369 --> 00:05:20,863
Not just higher prices, but people coming back.

118
00:05:20,863 --> 00:05:23,837
That's promise two, the pull-back power,

119
00:05:23,837 --> 00:05:25,770
showing up in the numbers.

120
00:05:26,580 --> 00:05:31,170
And customer balances aren't the only money Starbucks got up front.

121
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:32,486
In 2018,

122
00:05:32,486 --> 00:05:35,219
Nestle paid 7.15 billion dollars up front for

123
00:05:35,219 --> 00:05:38,680
the right to sell Starbucks beans and capsules in stores.

124
00:05:38,900 --> 00:05:40,006
Why so much up front?

125
00:05:40,006 --> 00:05:42,376
It's the price of using the Starbucks name in

126
00:05:42,376 --> 00:05:44,220
grocery aisles and coffee capsules.

127
00:05:44,430 --> 00:05:47,296
Starbucks spreads it as revenue over 40 years.

128
00:05:47,296 --> 00:05:49,850
Like a salary paid out over four decades.

129
00:05:50,680 --> 00:05:53,862
So the books still carry 5.6 billion dollars of

130
00:05:53,862 --> 00:05:55,690
long-term deferred revenue.

131
00:05:55,900 --> 00:05:59,028
Add the 1.75 billion in customer balances:

132
00:05:59,028 --> 00:06:02,380
Starbucks is very good at getting paid first.

133
00:06:03,200 --> 00:06:05,020
So how should we look at the stock?

134
00:06:05,840 --> 00:06:08,669
Let's borrow the method of Warren Buffett's teacher,

135
00:06:08,669 --> 00:06:09,540
Benjamin Graham.

136
00:06:09,760 --> 00:06:11,695
Take average earnings over several years,

137
00:06:11,695 --> 00:06:13,110
and pay at most 10 times that.

138
00:06:13,330 --> 00:06:17,710
Count future growth as zero. A strict floor.

139
00:06:18,530 --> 00:06:19,513
By this math,

140
00:06:19,513 --> 00:06:22,235
one Starbucks share is worth $26.16.

141
00:06:22,235 --> 00:06:25,260
At the Oct 23 close it traded at $100.8.

142
00:06:25,480 --> 00:06:28,256
The market pays 3.9x the calculator,

143
00:06:28,256 --> 00:06:31,726
partly because fiscal 2025 earnings per share

144
00:06:31,726 --> 00:06:33,500
fell by more than half.

145
00:06:33,720 --> 00:06:36,690
The market is betting Niccol's turnaround keeps going.

146
00:06:37,510 --> 00:06:40,814
The company sounds confident: it guided fiscal

147
00:06:40,814 --> 00:06:43,830
2026 adjusted EPS to 2.55 to 2.65 dollars.

148
00:06:44,060 --> 00:06:46,987
That's over 20% above last year's 2.13.

149
00:06:46,987 --> 00:06:50,440
This report is the first test of that promise.

150
00:06:51,250 --> 00:06:52,689
If the recovery continues,

151
00:06:52,689 --> 00:06:56,121
more customers become profit and the multi-year average rises,

152
00:06:56,121 --> 00:06:57,450
lifting the floor value.

153
00:06:57,670 --> 00:06:58,476
If it stalls,

154
00:06:58,476 --> 00:07:01,203
the weak years drag the average down and the

155
00:07:01,203 --> 00:07:03,000
gap with market hopes widens.

156
00:07:03,830 --> 00:07:07,932
ValueNote runs this calculator daily on over 600 companies,

157
00:07:07,932 --> 00:07:11,130
Starbucks included. Its ValueNote score is 21.

158
00:07:11,350 --> 00:07:13,851
It's a floor that counts growth as zero,

159
00:07:13,851 --> 00:07:16,540
not a target price or a buy or sell signal.

160
00:07:17,360 --> 00:07:18,926
Finally, promise three.

161
00:07:18,926 --> 00:07:21,038
Results come out after the U.S.

162
00:07:21,038 --> 00:07:22,400
close on October 29.

163
00:07:23,220 --> 00:07:26,103
So if you own or are eyeing Starbucks,

164
00:07:26,103 --> 00:07:29,290
what should you watch? Just three numbers.

165
00:07:30,110 --> 00:07:31,960
One: U.S. comparable sales.

166
00:07:31,960 --> 00:07:35,250
The company guided to 6.5% or more this quarter.

167
00:07:35,470 --> 00:07:38,837
Two: transactions. Whether people, not prices,

168
00:07:38,837 --> 00:07:42,350
keep coming back shows if the string is holding.

169
00:07:42,570 --> 00:07:44,568
Three: operating margin.

170
00:07:44,568 --> 00:07:48,147
The company expects above 11% for the year,

171
00:07:48,147 --> 00:07:52,060
showing whether the store fixes are paying off.

172
00:07:52,880 --> 00:07:56,067
To sum up: interest is 4% of profit,

173
00:07:56,067 --> 00:07:57,660
breakage under 8%.

174
00:07:57,890 --> 00:07:59,961
What really holds Starbucks up is that app

175
00:07:59,961 --> 00:08:02,230
balance that brings you back tomorrow morning.

176
00:08:02,450 --> 00:08:06,164
One tip for your own money: load only what you'll spend in a month.

177
00:08:06,164 --> 00:08:08,270
The interest goes to Starbucks anyway.

178
00:08:09,090 --> 00:08:12,019
Curious how the three numbers land on October 29?

179
00:08:12,019 --> 00:08:14,530
Subscribe, like and turn on notifications.

180
00:08:14,750 --> 00:08:16,605
Watch our Tesla episode too,

181
00:08:16,605 --> 00:08:20,780
and you'll have one more way to read how a company makes money.

182
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:24,050
How much is left in your app right now?

183
00:08:24,050 --> 00:08:27,570
Tell us in the comments. Thanks for watching.
